<TopicPage slug="subjects/forensic-science/foundational/foundations-of-forensic-science">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Forensic science is applied science with a client: the courtroom. Its central premise is Edmond
    Locard's exchange principle — <strong>every contact leaves a trace</strong> — and its central
    discipline is making claims that survive hostile scrutiny. The science is only half the job;
    documentation, chain of custody and honest interpretation are the other half.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="Locard's principle, worked through a burglary" widget="equationStep" config={locardCfg} />

  <h2>The history is a history of identification</h2>
  <p>
    Each era's forensic revolution has been a better way to answer "who did this?": anthropometry,
    then fingerprints, then blood groups, then DNA profiling. Each method raised the bar for the
    last one — and DNA's near-uniqueness quietly exposed how much of the rest rested on judgement
    rather than statistics.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="from Galton's prints to the NAS report" widget="timeline" config={historyCfg} />

  <h2>Class versus individual characteristics</h2>
  <p>
    Every comparison in forensic science splits evidence into two tiers. <strong>Class</strong>
    characteristics place evidence in a group: this fibre is blue acrylic, this shoe is a size 10
    training shoe. <strong>Individual</strong> characteristics can tie evidence to a single source:
    random wear marks on a barrel, a full DNA profile. Much of the discipline's history is the
    story of methods drifting between those tiers — and the 2009 NAS report's warning that some
    had drifted without the statistics to support them.
  </p>

  <h2>The discipline's real skill</h2>
  <p>
    Forensic scientists mostly work with imperfect, degraded, mixed samples and must still say
    exactly what their methods can and cannot support. Modern practice is converging on
    likelihood-ratio language — how much more likely is this evidence if the suspect is the source
    than if they are not? That humility, plus rigorous documentation, is what separates science
    from theatre in the courtroom.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> FEPAC accreditation standards (AAFS); National Research Council, <em>Strengthening Forensic Science in the United States</em> (2009); Jackson &amp; Jackson, <em>Forensic Science</em>.</p>
